1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,390
Elias's newspaper was dated
tomorrow.

2
00:00:02,873 --> 00:00:03,703
He lived alone.

3
00:00:03,853 --> 00:00:04,883
He had no subscription.

4
00:00:05,325 --> 00:00:07,935
A misprint — until page nine
reported his

5
00:00:07,935 --> 00:00:09,767
death. Apartment fire.

6
00:00:09,977 --> 00:00:11,207
Friday. Three a.m.

7
00:00:11,207 --> 00:00:13,822
His name. He booked a hotel across

8
00:00:13,822 --> 00:00:16,407
town. Saturday's paper was at the
front

9
00:00:16,407 --> 00:00:18,774
desk. The fire happened anyway.

10
00:00:19,004 --> 00:00:20,594
Same building. Same hour.

11
00:00:20,644 --> 00:00:22,645
Different man. The dead man was
his

12
00:00:22,645 --> 00:00:24,705
neighbour, who'd knocked to warn
him.

13
00:00:24,939 --> 00:00:27,649
Elias spread every paper he'd
saved across

14
00:00:27,649 --> 00:00:29,816
the floor. All dated tomorrow.

15
00:00:29,986 --> 00:00:31,976
Each mentioned him less than the
last.

16
00:00:32,432 --> 00:00:33,442
First his job.

17
00:00:33,612 --> 00:00:34,402
Then his friends.

18
00:00:34,612 --> 00:00:35,662
Then the crossword.

19
00:00:35,999 --> 00:00:38,129
By Friday, his photograph was a
grey

20
00:00:38,129 --> 00:00:41,221
rectangle. Saturday's paper
arrived blank.

21
00:00:41,391 --> 00:00:42,821
Only the masthead remained.

22
00:00:43,388 --> 00:00:46,078
The Midnight Archive: all the news
that

23
00:00:46,078 --> 00:00:49,412
never was. Elias finally
understood.

24
00:00:49,582 --> 00:00:51,312
It wasn't predicting his future.

25
00:00:51,637 --> 00:00:53,147
It was deciding what to keep.
